Corbett Foothills Retreat — Booking Page Design Specification
Version: 4.0
Date: 25 July 2026
Status: Design Specification (Pre-Implementation)
Page: https://corbett.life/booking/
1. Content & Data Architecture
Why This Separation Exists
A booking page is not a monolith. It is a merger of two distinct concerns:
Storytelling — the emotional, visual, and editorial layer that convinces a guest to book. This is the website's job.
Operations — the real-time availability, pricing, inventory, and transactional layer that completes a booking. This is the PMS's job.
These are fundamentally different domains with different lifecycles, different authors, and different technical requirements.
Room photographs change when the interior designer refreshes a suite. Marketing copy evolves as the brand voice matures. SEO content is tuned seasonally. None of this should require a PMS update.
Conversely, nightly rates change daily. Availability shifts with every reservation. Tax rules update with government policy. None of this should require a website redeploy.
InnPilot is a PMS and Booking Engine. It is not a CMS. It should never be asked to store, serve, or manage website presentation content. Its responsibility is operational: availability, pricing, packages, taxes, occupancy rules, booking transactions, and reservation management.
Corbett.life is the marketing website. It owns all presentation content: room photography, marketing copy, amenity lists, descriptions, hero imagery, testimonials, trust content, SEO metadata, and brand presentation.
The booking page sits at the intersection. It renders website content (what a room looks like, what it offers, why it's special) alongside InnPilot data (whether it's available, what it costs, what packages are offered).
This is not a design preference. It is a sound engineering principle.
Why InnPilot Remains CMS-Agnostic
InnPilot is designed to serve many properties. Each property has a different website, a different CMS, a different design system, and different content needs. If InnPilot owned presentation content, every property would need to:
- Upload images through the PMS interface (poor image management)
- Write marketing copy in a PMS text field (no rich editing)
- Manage SEO metadata in a booking engine (wrong tool)
- Redeploy their website when room copy changes (unnecessary coupling)
By keeping InnPilot focused on operations, any property can use any CMS — WordPress, Contentful, Sanity, Astro content collections, even static markdown files — and merge that content with InnPilot's booking data at the frontend layer.
InnPilot's API returns operational data. Presentation data comes from the website's own content layer.
Why This Makes InnPilot Reusable Across Hotels
A PMS that also functions as a CMS is a PMS that is tightly coupled to a single website implementation. By remaining operationally focused, InnPilot can be integrated into:
- A luxury resort's custom-built Astro site
- A boutique hotel's WordPress theme
- A chain's Next.js platform
- A property's simple static page
The integration contract is simple: Give me availability and pricing for these room slugs. I'll handle the rest.
How Websites Remain Completely Independent
The website has full control over:
- When and how room content is updated
- Which rooms are displayed and in what order
- How room cards are designed and laid out
- What imagery, copy, and SEO content appears
- How the booking flow is presented
- What trust signals, testimonials, and policies are shown
The website can be redesigned, rebranded, or migrated to a new framework without touching InnPilot. The only contract is the room slug and the API endpoints.
How Room Slugs Join Both Datasets
The room slug is the single join key between the website's content layer and InnPilot's operational layer.
Website Content (corbett.life)          InnPilot API
┌─────────────────────────────┐         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
│ slug: "forest-suite"        │         │ room_type_id: "fs-001"      │
│ photos: [...]               │         │ slug: "forest-suite"        │
│ description: "..."          │   ←→    │ available: true             │
│ amenities: [...]            │  slug   │ nightly_rate: 7000          │
│ bed_type: "King"            │         │ packages: [...]             │
│ area: "450 sq ft"           │         │ total: 21000                │
│ highlights: [...]           │         │ taxes: 2520                 │
│ seo: { ... }                │         │                             │
└─────────────────────────────┘         └─────────────────────────────┘
The frontend:
1. Loads website content for all rooms (from the site's content layer — markdown files, CMS API, or static data)
2. Calls GET /availability with dates and guests
3. Matches API response to website content using the slug field
4. Renders merged data: website content for presentation, API data for operations
5. If a slug from the API has no matching website content, it is rendered with API data only (graceful degradation)
6. If a slug from the website content has no matching API availability, it is shown as unavailable or hidden
How Future Headless CMS Support Would Work
Today, the website uses Astro content collections (markdown files in src/content/rooms/). This is the simplest approach for a static site.
If the website later migrates to a headless CMS (Contentful, Sanity, Strapi, etc.), the integration does not change:
1. The CMS serves room content via its own API
2. The frontend fetches CMS content and InnPilot availability
3. The slug join remains the same
4. No InnPilot changes required
The architecture is CMS-agnostic by design. The website's content source is an implementation detail that is invisible to the booking flow.
Why This Reduces Coupling
With Coupling Without Coupling
Changing room copy requires PMS update  Changing room copy requires website update only
Redesigning room cards requires PMS API changes Redesigning room cards requires frontend changes only
Adding a new room requires PMS + CMS + frontend Adding a new room requires website content + InnPilot inventory
Switching PMS requires rebuilding content layer Switching PMS requires only new API integration
PMS downtime affects content rendering  PMS downtime shows "check availability" but content remains visible
The booking page degrades gracefully: if InnPilot is temporarily unreachable, the website content still renders. The user sees room information and descriptions. They simply cannot check availability or complete a booking until the API responds.
Presentation Assets Are Progressive Enhancements
This is a foundational architectural principle of the booking page.
The booking page does not depend on any presentation assets being supplied by InnPilot. If corbett.life chooses to display room photographs, galleries, videos, or other marketing media, they are sourced exclusively from the website content layer.
The booking UI must function correctly regardless of whether presentation assets exist.
A room card without a photograph is still a room card. It shows the room name, description, amenities, pricing, and a select button. A missing image does not break the layout, does not hide the price, and does not prevent booking.
Presentation assets should be treated as progressive enhancements rather than operational dependencies. This means:
- The page renders fully functional room cards with or without images
- The booking flow completes successfully with or without gallery views
- Video content is optional enrichment, not a prerequisite for understanding the room
- Marketing photography enhances emotional engagement but does not gate operational access
- The layout adapts gracefully to whatever presentation content exists
Operational dependency: If absent, the feature is broken. Example: pricing, availability, the select button.
Progressive enhancement: If absent, the feature still works but is less rich. Example: room photograph, gallery carousel, video tour.
The booking page has zero presentation dependencies. Every piece of marketing media is an enhancement layered on top of a fully functional booking interface.
