21. Accessibility Recommendations
WCAG 2.1 AA Compliance
Requirement Implementation
Colour contrast All text meets 4.5:1 ratio minimum. Body text: dark earth (#3D2E1C) on white. White text on dark green: verified contrast.
Focus states  Visible focus ring on all interactive elements (2px offset, brand accent colour)
Keyboard navigation All interactive elements reachable via Tab. Logical tab order follows visual flow.
Screen reader labels  All form fields have associated labels. All buttons have descriptive text. All presentation elements have appropriate alt text if they exist.
Reduced motion  Respect prefers-reduced-motion. Disable skeleton shimmer, disable scroll animations.
Touch targets Minimum 48x48px on mobile for all interactive elements
Error announcements Use aria-live="polite" for validation errors and loading states
Skip navigation "Skip to main content" link (hidden until focused)
Specific Accessibility Notes
- Date picker: Use native <input type="date"> for maximum screen reader support. Custom date pickers require extensive ARIA work and often fail accessibility.
- Package checkboxes: Use <fieldset> and <legend> for grouping. Each checkbox has a clear label.
- Booking summary: Use aria-live="polite" so screen readers announce price changes when packages are toggled.
- Sticky elements: Use aria-label to describe the sticky bar purpose.
- Merged content loading: Announce to screen readers when pricing data populates: "Pricing loaded" via aria-live="polite".
- Missing presentation assets: If a room card has no presentation area, the card's accessible name is derived from the room name and description. The absence of presentation content does not create an accessibility gap — screen readers simply have fewer elements to announce, which is appropriate.
