29. Animation & Transitions
Principles
- Animations should feel natural, not mechanical
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion
- Duration: 150–300ms for micro-interactions, 300–500ms for page transitions
- Easing: ease-out for entering, ease-in for exiting
Specific Animations
Element Animation Duration  Trigger
Room card select  Border colour transition + scale 0.98 → 1.0 150ms User taps select
Package toggle  Checkbox fill animation 150ms User toggles package
Summary price update  Fade number change  200ms API quote response
Skeleton shimmer  Left-to-right gradient sweep  1.5s loop Loading state
Sticky bar appear Slide up from bottom  300ms Room selected
Bottom sheet expand Slide up + fade backdrop  300ms User taps summary bar
Error message Fade in + slight slide down 200ms Error occurs
Section scroll-in Fade up + slight translate (10px) 400ms On intersection
Pricing populate  Fade in from skeleton 300ms API response received
Content-to-API merge  Subtle fade on pricing area only  200ms API data arrives
Card layout adapt Smooth height transition when presentation assets load or unload  300ms Content state change
30. Performance Considerations
Critical Rendering Path
1. Hero and search bar must render in first paint
2. Room cards render with website content immediately (no API dependency)
3. Booking summary renders as skeleton until API responds
4. Trust strip, policies, FAQ load on scroll (lazy)
Two-Layer Performance Strategy
Layer Source  Load Strategy Perceived Speed
Page frame (header, hero, footer) Website build Immediate (static HTML) Instant
Room content (text, details)  Website content layer Immediate (static/CDN)  Instant
Pricing and availability  InnPilot API  After user search 200–800ms
Booking quote InnPilot API  After room selection  200–500ms
Booking confirmation  InnPilot API  After user books  500–2000ms
The user sees a fully rendered page before any API call is made. This is the primary performance advantage of the two-layer architecture.
Without presentation assets: The page renders even faster because there are fewer resources to load in the content layer. The compact card layout has no media to fetch. The page reaches interactive state sooner.
Lazy Loading Strategy
Element Lazy? Source  Enhancement Level
Hero area No  Website Enhancement
Search bar  No  Website Critical
Trust strip Yes Website Enhancement
FAQ Yes Website Enhancement
Footer CTA  Yes Website Enhancement
API Response Caching
- Availability responses: No caching (real-time data)
- Quote responses: Cache for 30 seconds (prevents duplicate calls during package toggling)
- Booking responses: No caching (transactional)
This specification is ready for engineering handoff. Every section maps directly to implementation decisions. The two-layer architecture — website content for storytelling, InnPilot for operations — is reflected consistently throughout. The progressive enhancement principle ensures that presentation assets enrich the experience without ever gating the booking flow. The design is presentation-agnostic: the booking page imposes no constraints on what the website's content layer provides, and functions completely regardless of what it provides. The design prioritises simplicity, transparency, and trust, while the architecture ensures that content and operations remain independent, maintainable, and resilient.